WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.913
Runtime choice affects supported model formats, acceleration backends, memory management,

00:00:03.913 --> 00:00:05.057
and tuning controls.

00:00:05.057 --> 00:00:05.707
Llama.

00:00:05.707 --> 00:00:09.520
cpp offers broad portability and direct control over quantized models.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:12.570
Ollama provides a convenient model and service workflow.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:15.621
LM Studio combines model discovery, local chat, configuration,

00:00:15.621 --> 00:00:18.290
and API serving in a desktop interface.

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:21.721
These options are effective for individual and small-team workloads.

00:00:21.721 --> 00:00:26.297
VLLM targets high-throughput serving on supported hardware and can benefit concurrent workloads,

00:00:26.297 --> 00:00:30.491
but its strengths may not matter for one local interactive user.

00:00:30.491 --> 00:00:34.686
Open WebUI is a front end rather than the inference engine;

00:00:34.686 --> 00:00:39.642
its responsiveness depends on the backend, network path, and model server beneath it.

00:00:39.642 --> 00:00:44.981
Additional layers are worthwhile only when they solve a real interface or operations requirement.

00:00:44.981 --> 00:00:48.412
Match the model format and quantization to the runtime.

00:00:48.412 --> 00:00:53.369
A model optimized for one loader may be unsupported or inefficient in another.

00:00:53.369 --> 00:00:55.657
Driver versions, acceleration libraries, runtime builds,

00:00:55.657 --> 00:00:58.326
and hardware architecture form a compatibility chain.

00:00:58.326 --> 00:01:01.376
Track known-good versions and verify acceleration after changes.

00:01:01.376 --> 00:01:06.333
Newer software may improve performance, but it may also change defaults, memory use,

00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:07.096
or stability.

00:01:07.096 --> 00:01:12.053
Choose the simplest runtime that provides the required model support, control, and interface.

00:01:12.053 --> 00:01:17.010
Benchmark candidates with the same model and workload, then include setup time, reliability,

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:19.679
logs, updates, and recovery in the decision.

00:01:19.679 --> 00:01:24.254
A stable runtime that starts predictably and produces repeatable results is often

00:01:24.254 --> 00:01:28.180
better than a more complex stack with a small peak-speed advantage.
